const unit11={
id:11,title:'Fat-soluble vitamins and cancer risk',headline:'Absorb with fat.<br>Store with care.',intro:'Follow vitamins A, D, E and K from digestion to function, then connect nutrition with evidence-based cancer risk across 12 topics.',quizNote:'All 30 source questions are included. Questions 14 and 29 are preserved as discussion items because fixed sun-exposure multipliers and “cancer-promoting hormone” labels are not universal scientific rules.',
lessons:[
['Fat-soluble vitamin absorption','Trace vitamins A, D, E and K from the gut to tissues.','<p>Fat-soluble vitamins dissolve into dietary lipids, join <b>mixed micelles</b> formed with bile and are absorbed in the small intestine. Inside enterocytes they are packaged into <b>chylomicrons</b>, which enter lymph before reaching blood.</p><p>Unlike most water-soluble vitamins, these vitamins are commonly stored in the liver or adipose tissue. Storage helps bridge low-intake periods but also means high supplemental intakes can accumulate.</p><div class="example"><b>Why fat matters</b><p>A salad containing carotenoids and vitamin K is better absorbed with some dietary fat, such as nuts, seeds, avocado or oil. The meal need not be high in fat.</p></div><p>Fat malabsorption, impaired bile flow and some medications can reduce absorption of all four vitamins.</p>'],
['Vitamin A forms and RAE','Distinguish retinoids from provitamin A carotenoids.','<p>Vitamin A includes the retinoids <b>retinol, retinal and retinoic acid</b>. Retinyl esters store and transport retinol. Plant foods also supply provitamin A carotenoids, especially beta-carotene, which the body can convert to retinol.</p><p>Food vitamin A activity is expressed as micrograms of <b>retinol activity equivalents (RAE)</b>. RAE accounts for the lower and variable conversion of carotenoids compared with preformed retinol.</p><div class="example"><b>Same colour, different chemistry</b><p>Sweet potatoes and carrots are rich in beta-carotene. Eggs and dairy contain preformed vitamin A. Both can contribute to vitamin A status, but not gram for gram.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminA-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: vitamin A and carotenoids</a></p>'],
['Carotenoids, food and absorption','Use colourful produce without turning colour into a cure claim.','<p>Carotenoids are plant pigments. Beta-carotene, alpha-carotene and beta-cryptoxanthin can form vitamin A; lutein, zeaxanthin and lycopene do not but may have other biological activities.</p><p>Sweet potatoes, carrots and cantaloupe are carotenoid-rich. Absorption improves when plant cell walls are disrupted by chopping or cooking and when some fat and bile are present.</p><p>Very high carotenoid intake can cause <b>carotenodermia</b>, an orange-yellow skin tint that is usually harmless and spares the whites of the eyes. It differs from jaundice.</p><div class="example"><b>Food first</b><p>High-dose beta-carotene supplements are not equivalent to eating vegetables and can raise lung-cancer risk in people who smoke.</p></div>'],
['Vitamin A, vision and immunity','Rebuild rhodopsin and recognize deficiency.','<p>In rod cells, light converts 11-cis-retinal to all-trans-retinal. To reform <b>rhodopsin</b>, trans-retinal must be converted back to the cis form and recombine with opsin.</p><p>Vitamin A also supports epithelial differentiation and immune function. Deficiency can cause night blindness, xerophthalmia, rough follicular skin and increased susceptibility to infection.</p><div class="example"><b>Clinical context matters</b><p>Bumpy skin in severe dietary restriction can suggest vitamin A deficiency, but it is not diagnostic. Essential-fatty-acid deficiency, other nutrient deficits and dermatologic conditions can look similar.</p></div><p>Preformed vitamin A can be toxic at high chronic doses and is teratogenic in pregnancy; supplements require care.</p>'],
['Vitamin D forms, sources and sunlight','Replace a fixed sun rule with individualized risk assessment.','<p>Foods and supplements contain two main forms: vitamin D2 (<b>ergocalciferol</b>) and vitamin D3 (<b>cholecalciferol</b>). Skin also makes D3 when UVB reaches 7-dehydrocholesterol.</p><p>Melanin reduces cutaneous vitamin D production, so people with darker skin may need more UVB exposure to make the same amount. But there is no safe universal multiplier: latitude, season, time, cloud, clothing, sunscreen, age and individual biology all matter.</p><p>UV exposure raises skin-cancer risk. Food and supplements can meet needs without deliberately increasing unprotected sun exposure.</p><div class="example"><b>Absorption</b><p>Dietary vitamin D joins bile-containing micelles, is absorbed with fat and enters chylomicrons.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminD-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: vitamin D</a></p>'],
['Vitamin D, calcium and bone','Separate mineralization defects from loss of bone mass.','<p>Vitamin D is hydroxylated in the liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D and mainly in the kidney to active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption.</p><p>With parathyroid hormone, it also helps maintain blood calcium by increasing renal reabsorption and, when needed, mobilizing calcium from bone.</p><table><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Core problem</th></tr><tr><td>Rickets</td><td>Defective mineralization in growing bone</td></tr><tr><td>Osteomalacia</td><td>Defective mineralization of adult bone matrix</td></tr><tr><td>Osteoporosis</td><td>Reduced bone mass and microarchitectural deterioration</td></tr></table><p>Vitamins A and D both influence gene expression and cell differentiation through nuclear receptors.</p>'],
['Vitamin E and antioxidant defence','Protect membrane lipids without overselling supplements.','<p>Vitamin E is a family of tocopherols and tocotrienols; alpha-tocopherol is the form used to meet human requirements. Vegetable oils, nuts and seeds are major food sources.</p><p>Vitamin E interrupts lipid-peroxidation chain reactions in membranes and lipoproteins. Excess is stored largely in adipose tissue, while the liver selects and resecretes alpha-tocopherol.</p><p>High-dose vitamin E supplements can interfere with vitamin K-dependent clotting and increase bleeding risk, especially with anticoagulant or antiplatelet drugs.</p><div class="example"><b>Antioxidant is a role, not a treatment promise</b><p>Vitamin E and carotenoids can participate in antioxidant defence, but isolated high-dose supplements have not consistently prevented cancer or cardiovascular disease.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminE-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: vitamin E</a></p>'],
['Vitamin K transport and activation','Link carboxylation with clotting and bone proteins.','<p>Vitamin K1 comes mainly from leafy green vegetables. Menaquinones are found in some fermented and animal foods and are also produced by gut bacteria, though the contribution of colonic production to requirements is uncertain.</p><p>After absorption, dietary vitamin K travels in chylomicrons to the liver. The liver incorporates vitamin K into lipoproteins for redistribution.</p><p>Vitamin K is a cofactor for <b>gamma-carboxylation</b>, adding a carbon-containing carboxyl group to glutamate residues. This lets clotting proteins bind calcium and function. Vitamin K also activates bone proteins such as osteocalcin.</p><div class="example"><b>Fibrin needs the pathway</b><p>The insoluble fibrin web forms downstream of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors. Deficiency therefore increases bleeding risk.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminK-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: vitamin K</a></p>'],
['Food sources and vitamin retention','Build a practical A-D-E-K plate.','<table><tr><th>Vitamin</th><th>Food examples</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Sweet potato, carrots, leafy greens, eggs, dairy</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Fatty fish, fortified milk or alternatives, eggs</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>Vegetable oils, nuts, seeds</td></tr><tr><td>K</td><td>Leafy greens, broccoli, some oils</td></tr></table><p>Fortified milk, sweet potatoes and spinach together supply useful amounts of at least three fat-soluble vitamins. Short cooking with little water, such as stir-frying broccoli, can preserve vitamins while improving palatability.</p><div class="example"><b>Vitamin K consistency</b><p>People taking warfarin should not eliminate leafy greens. They should keep vitamin K intake reasonably consistent and coordinate changes with their clinician.</p></div>'],
['Viruses and preventable cancer risk','Connect persistent infection with liver and cervical cancer.','<p>Some infections can cause or promote cancer. Persistent high-risk <b>HPV</b> causes virtually all cervical cancers and contributes to anal, penile, vulvar, vaginal and oropharyngeal cancers. Chronic <b>hepatitis B or C</b> can lead to liver cancer.</p><p>Vaccines prevent infection with cancer-causing HPV types and hepatitis B. Screening can find cervical precancer, and antiviral treatment can reduce complications of hepatitis.</p><div class="example"><b>Risk is not destiny</b><p>Most HPV infections clear. Cancer risk rises when high-risk infection persists and interacts with other factors such as smoking or immune suppression.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/infectious-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCI: infectious agents and cancer</a></p>'],
['Hormones and cancer: precise language','Replace “promoting” labels with tissue- and exposure-specific risk.','<p>Hormones regulate normal growth and function. Estrogen is not simply a “cancer-promoting hormone,” but some breast and endometrial cells respond to estrogen, and longer or unopposed exposure can increase risk in particular settings.</p><p>Risk depends on dose, duration, tissue, receptor status, life stage, genetics and whether progestin is present. For example, systemic estrogen alone increases endometrial-cancer risk in people with a uterus, while combined menopausal therapy has a different pattern of benefits and harms.</p><div class="example"><b>Use the full statement</b><p>Say “estrogen exposure can stimulate growth of hormone-sensitive tissue and influence risk” rather than treating a normal hormone as a carcinogen by itself.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/hormones/mht-fact-sheet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCI: menopausal hormone therapy and cancer</a></p>'],
['High-temperature meat and cancer evidence','Name HCAs and PAHs, then focus on exposure reduction.','<p>Cooking muscle meat at high temperatures can form <b>heterocyclic amines (HCAs)</b> when amino acids, sugars and creatine react. Grilling over flame can also form <b>polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs)</b> when dripping fat creates smoke that deposits on meat.</p><p>HCAs and PAHs are mutagenic and cause tumours in laboratory animals at high doses. Human observational evidence is less direct, so charred meat is a potential risk factor, not proof that one meal causes cancer.</p><div class="example"><b>Lower exposure</b><p>Avoid prolonged high heat, turn meat often, remove charred portions, prevent flare-ups and use lower-temperature methods more often. A varied pattern rich in plant foods matters more than one cooking trick.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/diet/cooked-meats-fact-sheet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCI: high-temperature cooked meat</a></p>']
],
questions:[
{n:1,q:'Following absorption, fat-soluble vitamins initially circulate in the _____ via _____.',o:['Lymph; chylomicrons','Lymph; transport proteins','Blood; LDL','Blood; triglycerides'],a:0,e:'They are packaged into chylomicrons in intestinal cells and enter lymph before reaching blood.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:2,q:'Which statement about fat-soluble vitamins is true?',o:['They leave the intestine directly through blood.','They circulate without other molecules.','They primarily act as energy-metabolism coenzymes.','They are typically stored in the body.'],a:3,e:'Fat-soluble vitamins can be stored in the liver or adipose tissue. They circulate in lipoproteins or with binding proteins.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:3,q:'Retinoids include:',o:['Retinyl, retinal acid and retinin','Retinase, retinin acid and retyne','Retinyl, retyne acid and retamin','Retinol, retinoic acid and retinal'],a:3,e:'Retinol, retinal and retinoic acid are the major functional retinoid forms.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:4,q:'What unit measures vitamin A activity in foods?',o:['Retinol activity equivalent (RAE)','Active retinol unit','Retinol functional equivalent','Functional retinol unit'],a:0,e:'Micrograms RAE account for differences between preformed retinol and provitamin A carotenoids.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:5,q:'Which foods are rich in carotenoids?',o:['Vegetable oils','Nuts, seeds and whole grains','Sweet potatoes, carrots and cantaloupe','Skim milk, eggs and yogurt'],a:2,e:'Orange and dark-green produce commonly supplies carotenoids such as beta-carotene.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:6,q:'Why can a very low-fat diet reduce vitamin A and carotenoid absorption?',o:['Digestive enzymes are scarce.','Digestive hormones are unavailable.','Protein carriers cannot be made.','Absorption requires dietary lipid and bile.'],a:3,e:'Dietary fat and bile help form micelles that carry fat-soluble compounds to the intestinal surface.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:7,q:'After light changes retinal, what must occur before rhodopsin can reform?',o:['Cis-retinal becomes trans-retinal.','Trans-retinal becomes cis-retinal.','Beta-carotene becomes preformed vitamin A.','Preformed vitamin A becomes beta-carotene.'],a:1,e:'All-trans-retinal must be regenerated to 11-cis-retinal before recombining with opsin.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:8,q:'What can result from vitamin A deficiency?',o:['Night blindness and increased infection risk','Rickets and balance loss','Hemolytic anemia and hearing loss','Bleeding and poor muscle control'],a:0,e:'Vitamin A supports rhodopsin, epithelial integrity and immune function.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:9,q:'Carotenoids can help protect membranes, DNA and proteins from:',o:['UVB rays directly','Inactivation during cooking','Free-radical damage','Loss through respiration'],a:2,e:'Some carotenoids participate in antioxidant defence by quenching reactive species.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:10,q:'Higher circulating carotenoid levels have been associated with lower risk of which retinal condition?',o:['Cataracts','Macular degeneration','Xerophthalmia','Retinal degradation'],a:1,e:'The source answer is age-related macular degeneration. Association does not prove carotenoid supplements prevent it.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:11,q:'Bumpy, rough, irritated skin in a severely restricted diet may suggest deficiency of vitamin:',o:['A','D','K','E'],a:0,e:'Vitamin A deficiency can cause follicular hyperkeratosis, but this skin finding is nonspecific and needs clinical assessment.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:12,q:'An orange skin tint in a toddler suggests checking high intake of:',o:['Carrots, tomatoes, cantaloupe and egg yolks','Whole milk, cheese and high-fat snacks','Vitamin A-fortified foods','Low-fat, low-fibre foods'],a:0,e:'Carotenoid-rich foods can cause harmless carotenodermia. Yellow eyes or illness requires medical assessment for jaundice.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:13,q:'How many main forms of vitamin D occur in foods and supplements?',o:['One','Two','Three','Four'],a:1,e:'The two main forms are D2, ergocalciferol, and D3, cholecalciferol.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:14,q:'Compared with light skin, how much more sun exposure does dark skin universally require for adequate vitamin D?',o:['Up to three times more','Twice as much','One-third less','One-half less'],a:0,e:'A is the intended historical course answer, but there is no universal safe multiplier. Skin pigmentation, season, latitude, age, clothing and other factors change production. This item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:15,q:'In the small intestine, dietary vitamin D joins _____ with help from _____.',o:['Blood; lipase','Blood; cholecalciferol','Micelles; bile','Micelles; lipase'],a:2,e:'Bile salts help form mixed micelles that carry vitamin D and other lipids to enterocytes.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:16,q:'To help regulate blood calcium, vitamin D can facilitate:',o:['More intestinal calcium absorption and less liver uptake','Liver phosphorus uptake and more kidney calcium loss','Less stomach calcium absorption and more bone storage','Calcium release from bone and decreased kidney excretion'],a:3,e:'D is the best offered answer. Active vitamin D also importantly increases intestinal calcium absorption.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:17,q:'Which vitamins are associated with stimulating cell differentiation?',o:['A and D','D and E','E and K','K and A'],a:0,e:'Vitamins A and D act through nuclear receptors that regulate gene expression and differentiation.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:18,q:'Osteomalacia is _____, whereas osteoporosis is _____.',o:['Bone demineralization; inadequate mineralization','Hereditary; diet related','Inadequate bone mineralization; loss of previously healthy bone mass','Adult disease; childhood disease'],a:2,e:'Osteomalacia is defective mineralization; osteoporosis is reduced mass and structural deterioration of previously mineralized bone.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:19,q:'Besides nuts and seeds, which food is especially rich in vitamin E?',o:['Mushrooms','Vegetable oils','Dairy products','Carrots and sweet potatoes'],a:1,e:'Vegetable oils are major sources of alpha-tocopherol and other vitamin E forms.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:20,q:'Where is excess vitamin E stored?',o:['Muscle','Skin','Adipose tissue','Liver'],a:2,e:'As a fat-soluble vitamin, vitamin E is stored substantially in adipose tissue.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:21,q:'Which nutrients participate in preventing oxidation and free-radical damage?',o:['Carotenoids and vitamin E','Vitamins A and D','Vitamin A and carotenoids','Vitamins D and E'],a:0,e:'Vitamin E interrupts lipid oxidation, and some carotenoids can quench reactive species.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:22,q:'Which is a symptom or risk of vitamin E toxicity?',o:['Hemolytic anemia','Kidney stones','Neuromuscular problems','Bleeding'],a:3,e:'High supplemental vitamin E can impair clotting and increase bleeding risk.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:23,q:'Which preparation would likely retain the most original vitamin K when served?',o:['Well-cooked collard greens','Refrigerated cole slaw stored for hours','Spinach salad left on a buffet for hours','Stir-fried broccoli'],a:3,e:'Short, rapid stir-frying limits prolonged exposure while preserving much of the vitamin K.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:24,q:'What organ repackages absorbed vitamin K into lipoproteins for distribution?',o:['Large intestine','Liver','Small intestine','Adipose tissue'],a:1,e:'Chylomicron remnants deliver vitamin K to the liver, which redistributes it in lipoproteins.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:25,q:'Vitamin K-dependent enzymatic reactions add what to target molecules?',o:['Carbon','Sodium','Calcium','Phosphorus'],a:0,e:'Vitamin K supports gamma-carboxylation, adding a carbon-containing carboxyl group to glutamate residues.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:26,q:'Which vitamin is required for proteins that produce the fibrin clot that halts bleeding?',o:['D','A','K','E'],a:2,e:'Vitamin K activates several clotting factors through gamma-carboxylation.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:27,q:'Which group supplies useful amounts of at least three fat-soluble vitamins?',o:['Whole grains, carrots and nuts','Strawberries, peaches and eggs','Tuna, peas and citrus juice','Fortified milk, sweet potatoes and spinach'],a:3,e:'This combination provides vitamin D from fortified milk, vitamin A activity from sweet potatoes and spinach, and vitamin K from spinach.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:28,q:'Which cancers are often linked to viral infection?',o:['Bone and kidney','Brain and pancreas','Colon and breast','Liver and cervical'],a:3,e:'Chronic hepatitis B or C can cause liver cancer; persistent high-risk HPV causes virtually all cervical cancers.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:29,q:'Which hormone is described by the source as “cancer promoting”?',o:['Progesterone','Estrogen','Testosterone','Serotonin'],a:1,e:'Estrogen is the intended textbook answer, but the label is oversimplified. Exposure can influence hormone-sensitive cancer risk depending on tissue, dose, duration and context. This item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:30,q:'Charring meat at high temperatures can form which compounds shown to cause cancer in laboratory animals?',o:['Heterocyclic amines','Nitrates','Nitrosamines','Carcinsamines'],a:0,e:'High-temperature cooking can form HCAs; grilling over flame can also form PAHs.',unscored:false,image:null}
],
cards:[['Fat-soluble vitamins','A, D, E and K.'],['Initial transport','Chylomicrons carry absorbed vitamins through lymph to blood.'],['Storage','The liver and adipose tissue store many fat-soluble vitamins.'],['Retinoids','Retinol, retinal and retinoic acid.'],['RAE','Retinol activity equivalent; expresses vitamin A activity.'],['Provitamin A carotenoids','Beta-carotene, alpha-carotene and beta-cryptoxanthin.'],['Rhodopsin regeneration','All-trans-retinal must return to 11-cis-retinal.'],['Vitamin A deficiency','Night blindness, epithelial changes and higher infection risk.'],['Carotenodermia','Harmless orange-yellow skin from high carotenoid intake.'],['Vitamin D forms','D2 ergocalciferol and D3 cholecalciferol.'],['Vitamin D activation','Liver forms 25(OH)D; kidney mainly forms active 1,25(OH)2D.'],['Osteomalacia','Defective mineralization of adult bone matrix.'],['Osteoporosis','Reduced bone mass and microarchitectural deterioration.'],['Vitamin E sources','Vegetable oils, nuts and seeds.'],['Vitamin E role','Fat-soluble antioxidant that interrupts lipid peroxidation.'],['Vitamin E excess','Can increase bleeding risk.'],['Vitamin K1','Phylloquinone, abundant in leafy green vegetables.'],['Vitamin K function','Cofactor for gamma-carboxylation.'],['Vitamin K and clotting','Activates proteins that bind calcium in the clotting cascade.'],['HPV','Persistent high-risk infection causes virtually all cervical cancer.'],['Hepatitis viruses','Chronic HBV or HCV infection can cause liver cancer.'],['Hormone-sensitive cancer','Risk depends on tissue, receptors, exposure and context.'],['HCA','Heterocyclic amines formed in high-temperature cooked meat.'],['PAH','Smoke-derived compounds that can deposit on grilled meat.']]
};
